4. MANDATORY SOURCE PUBLICATION

4.1. You must make the complete Corresponding Source of every Operational Modification, Derivative Work, or Combined Work available to the public.

4.2. Publication is required regardless of whether the Covered Work is:

(a) Distributed;

(b) Used privately;

(c) Used commercially;

(d) Operated on private infrastructure;

(e) Accessible through a network;

(f) Provided without charge; or

(g) Incorporated into a larger Covered Work.

4.3. Publication must occur before or no later than the first Operational Use of the modified Covered Work.

4.4. Modifications used exclusively for Development Testing do not trigger publication until they are placed into Operational Use or Conveyed.

4.5. When a Covered Work is Conveyed, its complete Corresponding Source must be available at the time of Conveyance.

4.6. Publication must include the complete Corresponding Source of the Covered Work, not merely patches, change descriptions, or differences from the original Software.

4.7. Source code must be provided in its preferred form for modification.

4.8. Intentional obfuscation, encryption, withholding of required build materials, or other measures intended to frustrate modification do not satisfy this Section.

4.9. Material changes to an operational Covered Work must be reflected in the published Corresponding Source no later than deployment of those changes.

4.10. The Public Source Location must permit access without payment, mandatory registration, identity verification, or discretionary approval.

4.11. Access may not be conditioned upon accepting additional contractual restrictions inconsistent with this License.

4.12. CAPTCHA challenges, rate limits, authentication requirements, or other technical restrictions must not materially prevent ordinary users from obtaining the complete Corresponding Source.

4.13. The Corresponding Source must remain publicly available throughout operation of the applicable version and for at least three years following its final Conveyance.

4.14. If the original Public Source Location becomes unavailable, You must establish a replacement location without unreasonable delay.

4.15. You must not knowingly provide incomplete, misleading, or materially outdated Corresponding Source.

4.16. You may not incorporate material into a Covered Work where contractual or licensing restrictions applicable to that material would prevent compliance with the source-publication obligations of this License.

4.17. Where third-party rights prevent publication of required Corresponding Source, You must obtain the necessary permissions or refrain from creating, Conveying, or operating the affected Covered Work.

4.18. Nothing in this Section requires disclosure of independently developed materials outside the scope of the Covered Work or information whose disclosure is prohibited by applicable law.

5. NETWORK SOURCE ACCESS

5.1. Any person interacting with a Covered Work through a network must be provided a clear opportunity to obtain its complete Corresponding Source.

5.2. For a Covered Work with a graphical web interface, a clearly labelled link reading "Source Code" or substantially equivalent wording must be provided in a persistent, readily discoverable location within the interface.

5.3. The link must direct users to the Public Source Location or to a page providing direct access to it.

5.4. A Covered Work may require authentication for access to its functionality. However, the Public Source Location itself must remain accessible without authentication, registration, or other restrictions prohibited by Section 4.

5.5. Where a Covered Work exposes only an application programming interface, the source location must be identified in publicly accessible API documentation or through an appropriately documented endpoint.

5.6. The source-access mechanism must identify the applicable software version or revision.

5.7. A link deliberately concealed, made misleading, or placed where ordinary users cannot reasonably discover it does not satisfy this Section.

5.8. Compliance with this Section does not authorize Third-Party Hosting.

