11. TERMINATION AND REINSTATEMENT

11.1. Any violation of this License automatically terminates the rights granted to You by the affected Licensor immediately upon occurrence of the violation.

11.2. For a first violation involving the applicable Licensor and Covered Work, You may qualify for provisional reinstatement by completing a Cure within thirty (30) calendar days after the Notice Date.

11.3. Both the thirty-day cure period and the sixty-day termination-election period begin on the same Notice Date.

11.4. If You complete a Cure within thirty days after the Notice Date, Your rights are provisionally reinstated upon completion of the Cure.

11.5. Notwithstanding Cure or provisional reinstatement, the Licensor may elect to terminate Your rights by dispatching written notice of that election within sixty (60) calendar days after the Notice Date.

11.6. The Licensor may exercise this election regardless of whether the violation was intentional, negligent, inadvertent, cured, or otherwise remedied.

11.7. Termination under Section 11.5 becomes effective upon successful dispatch of the termination election in accordance with Section 12.

11.8. If the violation is fully cured within thirty days and the Licensor does not exercise the termination election within sixty days, provisional reinstatement automatically becomes permanent upon expiration of the sixty-day period.

11.9. Failure to complete a Cure within thirty days results in continued termination without entitlement to automatic reinstatement.

11.10. Any subsequent violation involving the same Licensor and Covered Work results in immediate and permanent termination without notice or an additional cure period.

11.11. The Licensor may voluntarily reinstate terminated rights through express written authorization.

11.12. Discretionary reinstatement may be subject to additional lawful conditions.

11.13. The Licensor may reserve the right to revoke discretionary reinstatement at any time, provided that reservation is expressly stated in the written reinstatement authorization.

11.14. Where such a reservation exists, the Licensor may revoke reinstated rights by written notice.

11.15. Termination does not automatically terminate rights previously granted directly to downstream recipients who remain independently compliant.

11.16. Cure, reinstatement, or termination does not eliminate accrued liability for prior violations.

11.17. Rights arising independently under applicable law are not extinguished by termination of this License.

12. NOTICES

12.1. Notices under this License must be made in writing.

12.2. Notices may be delivered through any written communication method reasonably calculated to reach the intended recipient, including:

(a) Electronic mail;

(b) Direct messaging through an electronic communications platform;

(c) Postal mail or courier service;

(d) Written communications through an established business, organizational, or project communication system; or

(e) Any other written communication method reasonably capable of delivering the Notice to the intended recipient.

12.3. A Notice must be directed to an address, account, or contact method:

(a) Expressly designated for legal or licensing communications;

(b) Publicly identified as a contact method for the recipient;

(c) Previously used by the recipient for communications concerning the relevant Software; or

(d) Otherwise reasonably established as belonging to or being controlled by the intended recipient.

12.4. A public social media post, public communication channel message, or communication directed to an unrelated account does not constitute valid Notice solely because it is publicly accessible.

12.5. The Notice Date is the date of successful dispatch, not the date on which the recipient opens, reads, acknowledges, or responds to the Notice.

12.6. For electronic communications, successful dispatch requires evidence that the message was transmitted to the designated or reasonably established destination without a known delivery failure.

12.7. For physical mail, successful dispatch requires evidence of deposit with an appropriate postal or courier service, addressed to a designated or reasonably established mailing address.

12.8. A message returned as undeliverable, rejected by the destination service, or known not to have reached the designated destination does not establish successful dispatch.

12.9. The sender bears the burden of establishing successful dispatch and the Notice Date.

12.10. All periods are measured in calendar days. The Notice Date is day zero, and the applicable period expires at 23:59:59 in the time zone of the recipient's designated address or principal place of business, where reasonably ascertainable, or otherwise at 23:59:59 UTC.

12.11. If applicable mandatory law requires a different method of service, receipt, or calculation of time, that law prevails to the extent required.

